<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A written proof that <m>n^2 - 1</m> is divisible by 8 for every odd number <m>n</m> begins: ‘Let <m>n</m> be an odd number.’</p>
<p>Which of these could correctly be the next line? Select all that apply.</p>
<div><ol marker="(1)">
<li>Then <m>n = 2k + 1</m> for every integer <m>k</m>.</li>
<li>Then <m>n = 2k + 1</m>, where <m>k = 1</m>.</li>
<li>Then <m>n = 2k + 1</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.</li>
<li>Then <m>n^2 - 1 = 8k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.</li>
<li>Then <m>n = 2k - 1</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.</li>
</ol> </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Since <m>n</m> is odd, the definition of odd gives an integer <m>k</m> with <m>n = 2k + 1</m>. ‘For some integer <m>k</m>’ says that such a <m>k</m> exists, and the proof then works with it. The <m>k</m> depends on <m>n</m> (for <m>n = 11</m>, <m>k = 5</m>), so it is not a fixed number, and it is not ‘every integer’. Writing <m>n = 2k - 1</m> for some integer <m>k</m> works equally well: it describes the same numbers, and leads to <m>n^2 - 1 = 4k(k - 1)</m>, the same proof. So the third and fifth lines are correct.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>‘For every integer <m>k</m>’ would mean that <m>n</m> equals <m>1, 3, 5, \ldots</m> all at once.</p></li>
<li><p>‘Where <m>k = 1</m>’ makes <m>n = 3</m>, so the rest of the proof would be about one number only.</p></li>
<li><p><m>n^2 - 1 = 8k</m> is the conclusion. It may not be written down until it has been proved.</p></li>
</ul></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>(3), (5)</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>